 ### energy
 
-To be documented (TODO) - saves energy use information of nodes to file.
+Configure radio electrical consumption, read cumulative energy, or save energy history.
 
 ```shell
-energy [save] "<filename>"
-```
+energy snapshot
+energy model ["<configuration.json>"]
+energy save ["<filename>"]
+```
+
+`energy snapshot` returns a single JSON object, closing all open radio-state
+intervals at the current simulation time without advancing time. The root
+`total_j`, `disabled_j`, `sleep_j`, `tx_j`, and `rx_j` are **sums over current
+nodes**, in joules. `nodes` contains the same quantities per `node_id`, plus
+cumulative `state_times_us`. The object also includes `schema_version` (1),
+`time_us`, `model_name`, `model_revision`, and `scope` (`current_nodes`). Nodes
+are ordered by ID. Repeated reads at the same time are idempotent. Subtract
+snapshots to evaluate a window, keeping the node set fixed; deletion removes a
+node's contribution and recreating an ID starts new counters.
+
+`energy model` returns the active model as JSON; with a quoted path, it loads a
+configuration and returns the new model. Each model contains `name`,
+`disabled_mw`, `sleep_mw`, `rx_mw`, and `tx`: a nonempty, strictly increasing table
+of `{"dbm": -10, "mw": 15.9}` points. Values are electrical power in mW, **not**
+RF radiated power. Consumption is interpolated linearly in dBm and clamped at
+the endpoints. A one-point table is constant. Fields and numbers are validated;
+unknown or missing fields are rejected. A model change closes existing
+intervals with the old coefficients, preserves past energy and increments
+`model_revision`. Configure the model before evaluation for comparable rewards.
+
+Without an explicit profile, OTNS uses the `legacy-constant` consumption model.
+For a variable TX curve load `etc/energy-lab-example.json`, a **datasheet-based
+TI CC2652R approximation at 3 V**, with TX settings from -20 to +5 dBm. It uses
+typical chip currents, not measurements of a complete Thread device. Restrict
+algorithm actions to the profile range. Idle listening is charged as RX; Sleep
+means radio off. See [profile sources](../etc/energy-lab-example.md) and
+[energy model details](../energy/README.md).
+
+`energy save` retains the existing text formats in `energy_results/` and closes
+open intervals before writing. Per-node energies are in mJ. The network history
+and GUI report the **mean per current node**, in mJ; use the JSON
+snapshot for network totals. The history export includes a sample at the save
+time. This model accounts for radio states, including actual frame TX (ACKs and
+retries), not CPU, sensors, or whole-board consumption.
+
+Python provides `ns.energy_model(filename)` and `ns.energy_snapshot()` returning
+dictionaries. No log/file parsing or gRPC/protobuf regeneration is needed.
 
 ### exe
 
