new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..4fe46c02276a35e602a67e1e38b69e39c87d913c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/etc/energy-lab-example.md
@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+# TI CC2652R profile for the Thread laboratory
+
+The JSON in `energy-lab-example.json` is a simplified, datasheet-based profile,
+not a measurement of a complete board. Reference: Texas Instruments CC2652R,
+[SWRS207J, revision J](https://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/cc2652r.pdf), sections
+8.5, 8.6 and table 8-1 (printed pages 10, 11 and 32). Conditions: 3 V, typical
+room temperature, DC/DC enabled. Conversion: `P[mW] = 3 V * I[mA]`.
+
+| Nominal TX setting (dBm) | Typical current (mA) | Profile consumption (mW) |
+|---:|---:|---:|
+| -20 | 4.6 | 13.8 |
+| -15 | 4.9 | 14.7 |
+| -10 | 5.3 | 15.9 |
+| -5 | 5.9 | 17.7 |
+| 0 | 7.0 | 21.0 |
+| 1 | 7.3 | 21.9 |
+| 2 | 7.6 | 22.8 |
+| 3 | 8.2 | 24.6 |
+| 4 | 8.6 | 25.8 |
+| 5 | 9.2 | 27.6 |
+
+Other values: RX 6.9 mA → 20.7 mW; standby 0.94 µA → 0.00282 mW;
+shutdown 150 nA → 0.00045 mW.
+
+## Deliberate approximations
+
+- The published TX table characterizes the chip PA in BLE operation. Using it
+  for Thread on the same multiprotocol device is a didactic approximation, not
+  a claim of exact IEEE 802.15.4 current characterization.
+- Profile dBm are the nominal settings. The datasheet's measured RF output
+  differs slightly; that deviation is not modelled.
+- Standby/shutdown chip currents are proxies for radio Sleep/Disabled. The
+  simulator does not track the MCU's independent active/idle state, transition
+  energy or board peripherals. This remains a radio-state energy estimate.
+- The model interpolates between settings and clamps outside the table.
+  Restrict actions to the ten listed settings (or at least [-20, +5] dBm): the
+  energy table alone does not constrain simulated RF output.
+
+## Idle, RX and the hop tradeoff
+
+Listening with no packet arriving is RX, at the same cost as receiving. Radio
+Sleep is radio off. In particular, the text-export column `Idle (mJ)` means
+Sleep, not idle listening. The chip also has a separate MCU Idle mode; it is not
+represented by an additional radio state here.
+
+At low power TX costs less than RX. That is consistent with the reference data.
+For fixed-duration always-listening routers, energy is approximately
+`sum_i[P_RX*T + (P_TX(p_i)-P_RX)*T_TX_i]`. Consequently additional forwarding can
+lower this metric when it replaces more costly RX listening. Evaluate complete network energy rather than assigning a fixed cost per hop.
